trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Radiation history is negative, reducing concern for radiation-induced sarcoma or thyroid cancer. Family history is unrevealing for Lynch syndrome — though formal genetic counseling may still be warranted given young-onset colon cancer, it’s lower yield for *this* neck mass. Next, I must perform a targeted physical examination — specifically of the neck mass itself (location, size, consistency, mobility, tenderness, overlying skin changes) and regional lymph node basins (to assess for multifocality or adenopathy patterns). Also, examination of the head and neck mucosa (oral cavity, oropharynx, larynx if possible) is essential to rule out an occult primary — even in absence of symptoms. Since direct visualization may require specialized tools, I’ll start with what can be assessed clinically: symmetry, cranial nerve function (e.g., CN XI for trapezius strength), and palpation.

[TEST] Perform targeted physical examination:  
- Palpate the neck mass: record location (e.g., posterior triangle, midline, lateral), approximate size (cm), consistency (soft/firm/hard), mobility (freely mobile vs. fixed), tenderness, and overlying skin appearance (normal, erythematous, ulcerated, dimpled);  
- Palpate bilateral cervical, supraclavicular, and axillary lymph nodes;  
- Assess shoulder elevation strength (to screen for spinal accessory nerve [CN XI] involvement);  
- Inspect oral cavity and oropharynx for asymmetry, masses, or ulcerations.

